course: biology
lesson: 30
title: Snakes and lizards
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Snakes and lizards are reptiles with dry scaly skin; a lizard usually has four legs, while a snake has none and slides by pushing the sides of its body against bumps on the ground, its belly scales gripping like the tread on a trainer.
explain: A snake smells with its tongue: it flicks the forked tongue out, catches scent particles and carries them to a special organ in the roof of its mouth, and the two tips tell it which side the smell is stronger on.
explain: It can swallow an egg bigger than its own head because its two lower jaw halves are joined by stretchy tissue, not because it «unhinges» its jaw, and it grows by shedding its whole skin, inside out like a sock pulled off.
explain: In The Jungle Book, Kaa the python hypnotises people with spinning eyes; real snakes can't hypnotise anyone, they just have no eyelids, so their stare never blinks.
explain: And cartoon chameleons turn into tartan or vanish against any background; real chameleons change colour mostly to show their mood and control their temperature, not to hide.
fact: Snakes smell with their forked tongue, which carries scent to a special organ in the roof of the mouth and tells which side a smell is stronger on.
fact: Snakes shed their skin in one piece as they grow, starting at the lips, and their eyes turn cloudy blue just before.
fact: Snakes swallow food whole; the two halves of the lower jaw are joined by stretchy tissue, so the idea that they unhinge their jaws is a myth.
fact: Venomous snakes inject venom through fangs, mostly to catch prey, and bite people only when frightened or stepped on; in Britain the adder is the only venomous snake.
fact: Pythons and boas are constrictors that squeeze prey so its blood stops flowing, and the reticulated python, over 6 metres, is the longest snake.
fact: Pit vipers such as rattlesnakes have heat-sensing pits on their faces that find warm-blooded prey in total darkness.
fact: A rattle is made of hollow rings of keratin that click together as a warning; a ring is added at each shed, and snakes may shed several times a year, so the count doesn't tell the age.
fact: Snakes push their bodies against bumps on the ground to move, and pythons still have tiny leg bones left from ancestors with legs.
fact: Many lizards drop their tail when grabbed, and it keeps wriggling to distract the predator; the new tail has cartilage instead of bone.
fact: Gecko toes have millions of tiny hairs that stick by very weak forces between molecules, adding up to a grip strong enough to hang from a ceiling.
fact: A chameleon is a four-legged lizard, and its tongue can be longer than its body and shoots out in a fraction of a second, and chameleons change colour mostly to show mood and control temperature.
fact: The Komodo dragon, up to 3 metres long, is the biggest lizard; it lives on a few Indonesian islands, hunts deer and has venom glands in its lower jaw.
exercise: Why does a snake flick its forked tongue in and out? | To pick up smells and carry them to an organ in its mouth | To sting anything that comes too near it, just like a wasp does | To taste the air for rain | To cool down like a panting dog
exercise: What do the two tips of a snake's forked tongue help it do? | Tell which side a smell is stronger on | Grab two insects at once | Hear sounds from both sides | Hold its food still while it chews with its back teeth
exercise: Why do you find a snake skin inside out in the garden? | The snake crawled out of it in one piece as it grew | A bird turned it inside out | Snakes take their skin off every night before they go to sleep | The snake was eaten and only its skin was left
exercise: What happens to a snake's eyes just before it sheds? | They turn cloudy blue | They fall out and regrow | They turn a bright red | They close for a month
exercise: How can a snake swallow an egg bigger than its head? | Its lower jaw halves are joined by stretchy tissue | It unhinges its jaw and puts it back | It breaks the egg open with its long fangs first, then drinks it | It sucks the egg in like a straw
exercise: Why does a keeper's sign say "venomous snakes, do not touch"? | A frightened venomous snake may bite and inject venom | Venomous snakes spit fire | Touching makes the snake's venom run out | The snakes will catch your cold and could die from it
exercise: What do venomous snakes use their venom for mostly? | Catching prey | Attacking people on purpose | Digesting leaves | Making their skin shiny
exercise: How does a python kill its prey? | It squeezes so the blood stops flowing | It injects strong venom | It bites it many hundreds of times with tiny teeth | It drowns it in a river every time
exercise: Which is the longest snake in the world? | The reticulated python | The adder | The rattlesnake | The grass snake
exercise: How does a pit viper find a mouse in total darkness? | Its face pits sense the mouse's body heat | It can hear what the mouse is thinking about | It sees perfectly in the dark with its big eyes | It waits very quietly by the hole for the mouse to squeak
exercise: What is a rattlesnake's rattle made of? | Hollow rings of keratin | Small stones it has swallowed | Its old teeth kept in its tail | Dry bones of the prey it ate
exercise: How does a snake slide fast across the grass with no legs? | It pushes its body against bumps while belly scales grip | It rolls along like a wheel | It hops on its tail | It walks on tiny hidden legs tucked away under its belly scales
exercise: Why does a lizard drop its tail? | The wriggling tail distracts the predator while it escapes | It is too heavy to carry | It wants to grow a longer and more colourful one to show off | The tail was ill
exercise: How does a gecko walk up a window and across the ceiling? | Millions of tiny hairs on its toes stick by weak forces | Its feet have suckers like an octopus's arms to hold on to glass | It makes glue from its skin | It is so light it floats
exercise: How fast does a chameleon's tongue shoot out? | In a fraction of a second | In about ten whole seconds | Slowly, like a tape measure | It doesn't move, the insect flies in
exercise: What is the biggest lizard in the world? | The Komodo dragon | The chameleon | The gecko | The iguana
exercise: Where do Komodo dragons live in the wild? | On a few Indonesian islands | All over Africa, south of the Sahara | In the rainforests of Brazil | In the hot deserts of Australia
test: in jungle book kaa hypnotises mowgli with his eyes. can snakes actually do that? | No, snakes can't hypnotise; they just have no eyelids, so they never blink | Yes, all pythons can do it | Yes, but only at night | No, because snakes have no eyes at all, they only feel and smell
test: Count them: a snake, a lizard and a chameleon sit on a branch. How many legs altogether? | 8 | 12 | 4 | 10
test: My cousin found 8 rings on a rattle and says the snake is 8 years old. Is he right? | No, a ring is added at each shed, and snakes shed more than once a year | Yes, one ring grows each birthday | Yes, but only for desert rattlesnakes, as they grow one a year | No, rattles have no rings
test: A cartoon chameleon turns tartan on a picnic blanket to hide. What's the truth? | Chameleons change colour mostly for mood and temperature, not to hide | Chameleons can copy any pattern they sit on, even tartan and spots and stripes | Chameleons are always green | Chameleons change colour only when asleep
test: Why does a snake's tongue look scary even though it can't hurt you? | It isn't a sting; it is only for smelling | It is full of venom | It can grab your finger | It burns your skin like a nettle sting
test: gross a snake skin!! does that mean the snake's dead? | No, it shed its old skin and grew a new one under it | Yes, it's all that's left of a snake that died here last week | Yes, snakes shed only when they die | No, it's a skin a bird left there
test: A lizard dropped its tail to escape a cat. What is different about the tail that grows back? | It has cartilage inside instead of bone | It grows back exactly the same, with bone | It never grows back, so the lizard has no tail for life | It grows back as two tails
test: Pythons have tiny leg bones but no legs. What is the best explanation? | Their ancestors had legs, and the bones are left over | Pythons are growing legs right now | They are bones from mice they ate | Pythons will grow legs when they are old and too heavy to slide
test: A pit viper and a lizard are in a dark cage with a warm mouse. Which finds it first and why? | The pit viper, because its pits sense heat | The lizard, because it can see perfectly well in the dark | Neither, mice give off no heat | Both at once, by hearing
test: You're walking on a British heath and see an adder. What's the sensible thing to do? | Leave it alone and walk round it; it bites only if disturbed | Pick it up, adders are harmless | Stamp near it to scare it off | Poke it with a long stick to see it move and hiss
test: A gecko's hair-like toes stick by very weak forces. How can such weak forces hold it on a ceiling? | Millions of tiny hairs add up to a strong grip | Each single hair is very strong on its own, like a hook | The ceiling pulls it up | The gecko is lighter than air
test: Why is a snake's way of eating whole a good fit for an animal that can't chew? | Stretchy jaws let it swallow big prey in one piece | It chews its food slowly with its rough tongue instead of teeth | It eats only liquid food | It cuts food with its scales
exam: Identify the organ by means of which a snake principally gathers scents from its surroundings. | The forked tongue, conveying particles to an organ in the palate | The nostrils alone | The eyes | The scales of the belly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mechanism whereby a snake ingests prey larger than its head? | The halves of the lower jaw are joined by elastic tissue that stretches | The jaw is detached from the skull and later reattached when it has finished | The prey is first chewed into portions | The prey is dissolved externally
exam: Ascertain the principal purpose for which the chameleon alters its colouration. | The signalling of mood and the regulation of temperature | Concealment against every possible background in which it finds itself | The attraction of rainfall | The deterrence of the Sun
exam: It is given that a lizard's tail is seized by a predator. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the outcome. | The tail may be shed and continue to wriggle while the lizard escapes | The lizard invariably perishes | The tail becomes venomous | The predator is thereby hypnotised and forgets the lizard
